A dripping faucet in the bathroom is usually caused by a worn washer, a damaged O-ring, mineral buildup, or a loose cartridge. The first thing to do is shut off the water supply at the shut-off valves under the sink; if those don’t stop the flow, close the main house valve and still run the faucet to relieve pressure. Then place a towel or small bowl under the faucet and remove the handle carefully so you can inspect the internal parts.

Use the appropriate screwdriver or wrench to take apart the faucet and check the cartridge, stem, or compression pack for wear. If you see corrosion or mineral buildup, clean the parts with a brush and vinegar, and make sure the stems aren’t scraping where they should move smoothly. If the valve is a faucet cartridge type and it looks okay, grease the O-rings lightly before reassembly, and if the faucet uses a separate air gap or trim ring, look for debris there too. For a compression-style faucet, replace the washer and any worn O-rings if they are flattened or cracked.

After cleaning or replacing the worn parts, reassemble the faucet in the order you took it apart and open the water supply slowly. Test by running it on both hot and cold to see if the drip stops;
